.Dd April 6, 2026
.Dt ZFSSUM 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfssum ,
.Nm zfssum-server
.Nd generate file fingerprints from ZFS block pointer checksums
.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfssum
.Op Fl l
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Op Fl a Ar algo
.Op Fl S
.Op Fl Z
.Ar dataset
.Ar path Op Ar path ...
.Pp
.Nm zfssum-server
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Op Fl m Ar mode
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Op Fl g Ar gid
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm zfssum
generates a content fingerprint for files stored on a ZFS dataset by
hashing the on-disk block pointer checksums rather than reading actual
file data.
This makes it significantly faster than traditional tools such as
.Xr sha256sum 1
for change detection, especially on large files.
.Pp
ZFS stores a checksum for every data block in its block pointer tree.
.Nm
walks the tree for each named file, collects the leaf-level block
checksums, and hashes them together into a single digest.
Because the leaf checksums depend on file content
.Pq via the pool's checksum algorithm ,
any change to the file data produces a different
.Nm
output.
.Pp
.Nm zfssum-server
is a persistent daemon that keeps the pool open across requests,
eliminating the per-invocation cost of
.Fn objset.open .
.Nm zfssum
connects to a running
.Nm zfssum-server
by default.
Use
.Fl l
to operate directly against the pool without the server
.Pq slower per invocation .
.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss zfssum options
.Bl -tag -width "--skip-holes"
.It Fl a , -algo Ar algo
Hash algorithm used to combine block checksums.
Supported values:
.Bl -tag -width "blake3" -compact
.It Cm blake3
.Pq default
BLAKE3, producing a 256-bit digest.
.It Cm sha256
SHA-256, producing a 256-bit digest.
.It Cm sha512
SHA-512, producing a 512-bit digest.
.It Cm bp
Root block pointer mode.
Outputs the
.Vt blk_cksum
stored in the dnode's root block pointer directly, without walking the
block tree.
This is the fastest mode but the hash may change after
.Nm zfs Cm send | Nm zfs Cm receive
for multi-level files because the root checksum covers indirect block
metadata
.Pq DVAs, birth TXGs
in addition to data checksums.
When the dnode has multiple root block pointers or the root BP is
embedded, the checksums are combined with BLAKE3.
The
.Fl S
and
.Fl Z
options are ignored in this mode.
.El
.It Fl l , -local
Local mode.
Operate directly against the pool via
.Sy libzpool
instead of connecting to
.Nm zfssum-server .
Incurs
.Fn kernel_init
overhead on every invocation.
.It Fl s , -socket Ar socket
Path to the Unix domain socket of
.Nm zfssum-server .
Default:
.Pa /var/run/zfssum.sock .
.It Fl S , -no-size
Do
.Em not
include the file size in the hash.
By default the file size
.Pq obtained from the SA attribute ZPL_SIZE
is fed into the hash context before the block checksums.
Including file size prevents theoretical collisions between files of
different sizes whose block checksums happen to match.
.It Fl Z , -skip-holes
Skip holes instead of filling zeros.
By default, 32 zero bytes are fed into the hash for each level-0
hole block pointer
.Pq Dv BP_GET_BIRTH Ns () Li == 0
so that sparse files with different hole layouts produce different
digests.
With
.Fl Z ,
holes are silently skipped
.Pq the original behavior .
.El
.Ss zfssum-server options
.Bl -tag -width "--mode MODE"
.It Fl d , -daemon
Daemonize after creating the server socket.
.It Fl s , -socket Ar socket
Path for the Unix domain socket.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/zfssum.sock .
.It Fl m , -mode Ar mode
Set the permission mode of the Unix domain socket, specified as an
octal value.
Default:
.Li 0666 .
.It Fl u , -uid Ar uid
Set the owner of the Unix domain socket to the specified numeric UID.
By default the socket is owned by the user running the server.
.It Fl g , -gid Ar gid
Set the group of the Unix domain socket to the specified numeric GID.
By default the socket inherits the group of the user running the server.
.El
.
.Sh PROTOCOL
.Nm zfssum-server
listens on a Unix stream socket.
Each client connection may send multiple requests, one per line.
.Ss Request format
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<dataset>\\t<path>[\\t<opts>]\\n
.Ed
.Pp
The optional third field
.Ar opts
is a comma-separated string of algorithm and flag tokens:
.Bl -tag -width "skipholes" -compact
.It Cm blake3 , sha256 , sha512 , bp
Algorithm selection (default:
.Cm blake3 ) .
.It Cm size , nosize
Include or exclude file size in hash (default:
.Cm size ) .
.It Cm holefill , skipholes
Fill zeros for holes or skip them (default:
.Cm holefill ) .
.El
.Pp
When the third field is absent, the server uses
.Cm blake3,size,holefill .
.Ss Response format
.Bd -literal -offset indent
OK <hex-digest>\\n
ERR <message>\\n
.Ed
.
.Sh CAVEATS
The digest produced by
.Nm
depends on the pool's storage configuration.
The same logical file content stored with different settings will
produce different digests:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Sy Checksum algorithm
.Pq e.g., sha256 vs blake3
\(em different algorithms produce different per-block checksums.
.It
.Sy Record size
.Pq e.g., Cm recordsize Ns = Ns 128K vs 1M
\(em different block boundaries yield a different set of checksums.
.It
.Sy Compression
.Pq e.g., lz4 vs zstd vs off
\(em ZFS checksums are computed on post-compression data.
.El
.Pp
This is inherent to the approach of reusing on-disk checksums.
Digests are meaningful for comparison only within the same dataset
or between datasets with identical storage properties.
.Pp
For embedded block pointers
.Pq very small files whose data is stored inside the block pointer itself ,
only the 14 payload words are hashed; the metadata words
.Pq Vt blk_prop , Vt blk_birth_word Ns [1]
are excluded so that the digest remains stable across
.Nm zfs Cm send | Nm zfs Cm receive .
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Generate a fingerprint using the default algorithm (BLAKE3):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfssum tank/data path/to/file
a1b2c3d4...
.Ed
.Pp
Use SHA-512 in local mode without the server:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfssum -l -a sha512 tank/data myfile
e5f6a7b8...
.Ed
.Pp
Fast root block pointer hash for change detection:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfssum -a bp tank/data largefile
1234abcd...
.Ed
.Pp
Start the server as a daemon with group-writable socket:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfssum-server -d --mode 0660 --gid 1000
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sha256sum 1 ,
.Xr b3sum 1 ,
.Xr zdb 8 ,
.Xr zfs 8
